<section class="section section--tight" aria-labelledby="types-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="types-title">How the options usually compare</h2>
      <p>A simplified starting point. Each row has exceptions, and tax outcomes depend on your numbers, so take advice before deciding.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Entity comparison, scrolls sideways on small screens">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Entity</th><th scope="col">People needed</th><th scope="col">Personal liability</th><th scope="col">Ongoing burden</th><th scope="col">Often chosen for</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Sole proprietorship</th><td>One owner</td><td>Unlimited</td><td>Lowest</td><td>Starting alone, with low risk</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Partnership firm</th><td>Two or more</td><td>Generally unlimited</td><td>Low to moderate</td><td>Small shared businesses</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">LLP</th><td>Reported as two designated partners</td><td>Generally limited</td><td>Moderate, with annual MCA filings</td><td>Professional and shared firms wanting limited liability</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">One Person Company</th><td>Reported as one director and a nominee</td><td>Generally limited</td><td>Higher</td><td>A solo founder wanting a company form</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Private limited company</th><td>Reported as at least two directors and two shareholders, with a resident director</td><td>Generally limited</td><td>Highest: audit, annual filings, meetings and records</td><td>Raising investment, or building a larger business</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
    <p class="section-foot">Requirements as reported by secondary guides. {% ext "https://www.mca.gov.in/", "MCA" %} is the official source, which we could not read directly.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="ask-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="ask-title">Questions that usually decide it</h2>
      <dl class="fit-list">
        <div><dt>Will you raise outside investment?</dt><dd>Investors often expect a company. An LLP or proprietorship may not suit them.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>How much risk could fall on you personally?</dt><dd>Limited liability has limits, for example if you personally stand surety for a loan.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Who will handle yearly compliance?</dt><dd>Budget for it from the start. Missed filings can bring penalties and consequences for directors.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Do your clients expect a particular form?</dt><dd>Some larger customers prefer a registered entity.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Separate the charges</h2>
      <p>Ask for a written split of any quote:</p>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Government filing fees and stamp duty</li>
        <li>Digital signature costs</li>
        <li>The professional's own fee</li>
        <li>What comes after: GST, bank account, first-year compliance</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="para">Very low "all-inclusive" prices may leave out one or more of these. Fees depend on capital and state, so check MCA's fee calculation for your case.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="flow-title">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="flow-title" class="first-h2">What the process involves</h2>
      <p>Companies are generally formed through the SPICe+ web form on the MCA portal, and directors need digital signatures. In outline:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Decide the entity and the people involved.</li>
        <li>Get digital signatures, and choose possible names.</li>
        <li>Prepare the incorporation documents and proof of the registered office.</li>
        <li>File, and answer any questions from the Registrar.</li>
        <li>After incorporation, handle items such as the bank account, tax registrations and the first compliance steps.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Timelines depend on the portal and the quality of documents. No one can promise a name or an approval.</p>

      <h2 id="who">Who does what</h2>
      <p>Company secretaries, Chartered Accountants, cost accountants and advocates are commonly involved in incorporation, and some forms may need a practising professional's certification. We did not read the rules that state which, so ask the professional which certification applies and in what capacity. An accountant without those qualifications may help with bookkeeping and tax after you form the entity, but may not be able to certify filings.</p>

      <h2 id="yours">What stays with you</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The truth of what is filed. Directors and partners sign for it.</li>
        <li>Your name on the digital signature. Keep control of its use.</li>
        <li>Ongoing duties, whoever you appoint to help with them.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Choosing a company only because it sounds more established</li>
        <li>Using a friend as a dummy director or partner, who then carries real duties</li>
        <li>Forgetting that an inactive company can still have filing duties</li>
        <li>Using an address without proper permission or documents</li>
      </ul>

      <p>After registration, see {% routeLink "/services/roc-mca-compliance/", "ROC and MCA compliance" %} and {% routeLink "/services/gst-registration/", "GST registration" %}.</p>

